 \ NVRAM variables
 \ --------------------------------------------------------
 \ fcode-debug? input-device output-device
+\ x86: auto-boot is unconditional (main.fs has no interrupt window) and the
+\ post-probe boot attempt detonates when IDE media is attached — land at the
+\ ok prompt instead; boot is typed there. Other arches keep the 1275 default.
+\ Spike 1: amd64 inherits this for the same reason x86 needs it -- it is the
+\ same main.fs with no interrupt window, and the trampoline lands in the same
+\ post-probe boot attempt. Without it the 64-bit firmware prints its banner,
+\ tries to boot nothing, and stops WITHOUT a prompt, which reads exactly like
+\ a hang and is not one.
+[IFDEF] CONFIG_X86
+s" false"    s" auto-boot?"           bool-config   \ 7.4.3.5
+[ELSE]
+[IFDEF] CONFIG_AMD64
+s" false"    s" auto-boot?"           bool-config   \ 7.4.3.5
+[ELSE]
 s" true"     s" auto-boot?"           bool-config   \ 7.4.3.5
+[THEN]
+[THEN]
 s" boot"     s" boot-command"         str-config    \ 7.4.3.5
 s" "         s" boot-file"            str-config    \ 7.4.3.5
 s" false"    s" diag-switch?"         bool-config   \ 7.4.3.5
@@ -342,6 +358,35 @@ s" true"     s" vga-ndrv?"            bool-config
 [IFDEF] CONFIG_X86
 \ ---- X86 ----
 s" true"     s" little-endian?"       bool-config
+\ Every other arch defines load-base; x86 never did, so $load's
+\ `" load-base" evaluate` executed an undefined word and crashed the
+\ auto-boot path (GPF at pc=0) as soon as a disk was attached.
+s" 4000000"  s" load-base"            int-config
+[THEN]
+
+[IFDEF] CONFIG_AMD64
+\ ---- AMD64 ----
+\ The per-arch section below had PPC, X86, SPARC32 and SPARC64 arms and no
+\ amd64 one (the only CONFIG_AMD64 test in this file was Spike 1's auto-boot?
+\ arm further up), so the 64-bit firmware got neither of these -- and
+\ `load-base` being undefined is not cosmetic: it is
+\ the SAME defect the x86 comment above describes, reached the same way.
+\ `boot <file>` that fails falls through to "Trying disk...", whose $load runs
+\ `" load-base" evaluate`, and an undefined word there ends the boot path in
+\ a Stack Underflow rather than an error anyone can read.
+\
+\ NOTE WHAT IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* COPIED FROM x86: arch/x86/openbios.c also
+\ feval()s a `constant load-base` over the top of this one, because x86
+\ relocates itself by rebasing the GDT and every Forth address is therefore
+\ segment-relative -- a constant PHYSICAL address is wrong there by
+\ construction. amd64 does not relocate (long mode ignores segment bases;
+\ arch/amd64/segment.c sets virt_offset = 0 and says so), so phys_to_virt()
+\ is the identity and this value is already correct. Transcribing x86's
+\ workaround would have added a second definition -- x86 prints "load-base
+\ isn't unique." on every boot because of it -- to fix a cause that does not
+\ exist here.
+s" true"     s" little-endian?"       bool-config
+s" 4000000"  s" load-base"            int-config
 [THEN]
 
 [IFDEF] CONFIG_SPARC32
