+\ treats 0 as "nobody wrote this" was asking a question the storage could not
+\ answer.
+\
+\ Measured 2026-08-26, and it is exactly why `go` hung on x86 and not on amd64:
+\
+\     load /ide@1/cdrom@0:\go.fth   then   load-state >ls.file-size @
+\       amd64  ->  0            ( so libopenbios/initprogram.c fell back to
+\                                 load-size, 31 bytes, and it worked )
+\       x86    ->  30000000     ( so it did NOT fall back, and handed
+\                                 `evaluate` 768 MB to interpret as Forth )
+\
+\ Same firmware, same file, same 31 bytes correctly loaded on both. The only
+\ difference was what happened to be lying in the dictionary at that address.
+\ amd64 had been passing BY LUCK.
+load-state load-state.size erase
+
 variable state-valid
 0 state-valid !
 
 variable file-size
 
-: !load-size file-size ! ;
+: !load-size  ( n -- )
+  \ BOTH RECORDS OF THE ONE FACT. `variable file-size` and
+  \ `load-state >ls.file-size` are two places holding the same number, and
+  \ before this only the first was written here while libopenbios reads the
+  \ second -- so a file loaded through $load reported its size to nobody who
+  \ was asking. Writing both at the point the fact is learned is what makes
+  \ the erase above mean something: 0 now says "nothing was loaded", not
+  \ "nothing wrote it down".
+  \
+  \ Ordering is safe: $load calls !load-size BEFORE init-program, so a C
+  \ loader that later fills in its own authoritative value still wins.
+  dup file-size !
+  load-state >ls.file-size !
+;
 
